A||||19||Cooter||A question for school kids, people with school kids or other teachers.||||1||
Z||000000||Cooter||08-13-2000||06:53 PM||||My system is weighing the pro's/con's of an extended school calendar- year round school. Does anybody have any experience- good or bad- with the extended school year. If I am asked to address our school board, I would like to have some information to present to them other than just my lame opinion.<P>Thanks,<BR>Greg||||reg||1
Z||000001||DaRkLoRD||08-13-2000||07:32 PM||||I'm not really a "school kid" anymore, unless you include college students...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>but I do think year round school is a good idea.. as long as you give the kids enough time off throughout the year so they don't get burned out.. For example: 2-3 weeks off in the summer, a couple weeks in the winter.. a week off in early spring, and another in the fall.. that's the same amount as most summer vacations, yet the kids are going to school all year round and don't have 2 months off during the summer to forget everything.. (this is less time off than what kids get now, if you take christmas into consideration as well as spring break) Obviously national and religious holidays would still be time off, but that's still only another week or so of combined "vacation" time.<P>Now.. there are some flaws with this, such as prople who enjoy going on long vacations in the summer.. or kids who work all summer. the vacation part couldn't really be helped if the school board decided to have year round schooling, but if they did that, then the school days themselves should be shortened, so kids could still have an after school job. This would work very well with elementary and jr. high schools, but not as well with high schools as many of the students there need to work full time jobs in the summer to be able to afford college, or a car, etc.<P>comments?<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A> <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000002||Cooter||08-13-2000||10:04 PM||||From what I have seen, the total number of days in school are the same, but at the end of every nine weeks, you get a couple of weeks off. Christmas vacation is 3 weeks instead of two, two weeks for spring break and a short summer vacation. I think I would like it, but would like more info from people who have actual experience with it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||2birds||08-13-2000||10:36 PM||||My oldest is just starting 1st grade, but they started on August 11, with the plan of ending in May, instead of mid-June, counting time off for hurricanes, etc.  I grew up in Iowa, home of the highest literacy rate in the country, at least back in the 70's and 80's.  We always started the week before Labor Day, and finished in the end of May.  Never June.<BR>My thoughts on this, and they are just that, are these:<BR>Are the parents for this just so they can have year-round child care for free, so Mom and Dad can both work?  That's usually the first response I hear from the parents I've talked to.  I've got news, and it's called more teacher work days.  I haven't heard any talk of adding more classroom days to the schedule, except for snow or hurricane days.  Most of the reasoning I've heard from the teachers is more lesson preparation time.  With the increased results teachers are expected to get from their not-always-prepared students, I can see the need for more time.  <P>I can see the need for the teachers to work (and get paid more for it!) year round, but the kids (especially the younger ones) need the summer break.  You're only a kid once, and it gives them something to look forward to.  Has the adult world forgotten this?  My son was so excited about graduating from Pre-K and Kindergarten, and even more excited about starting the new year.  I think this would be greatly diminished by just a two or three week break.  Summer has always been the traditional family vacation time (at least with my family).  Has that gone away, too?  And just because thay aren't in school, doesn't mean they have to stop learning.<BR> <BR>Bottom line is, at least IMHO, let's not rely on the schools to replace what our families have traditionally done.  Pay the teachers what they are worth, considering the results we are expecting, and don't rely on them to replace us in our parental roles.  Our responsibilty doesn't end when they are potty-trained and day-care ready.  Keep a close eye on their school activities, so they and their teachers know you care, and get involved with their learning.  I love helping my kids discover something new.  When you go to sea for a few months at a time, you see how much they change so quickly, and it tears you up that you can't always be there for it as it happens.  Your kids only stay little for so long, and even when they are older, they still need to know you care... <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Taijiguy||08-14-2000||09:03 AM||||I think you have a point 2Birds, in that parents have to be more responsible in taking care of their children, The problem is, that unless there's a stay-at-home parent, the kid gets no more exposure to family than if they were in school. <BR>Most people don't realize that the school "year" wasn't designed to provide children time off between grades. The concept of a split year was created to allow the kids to be at home during peak farming times. (Over time, the "break" as been moved so it falls during the summer, it originally fell later in the year) So they could be at home to help tend the farm and work the fields with the rest of the family. So unless a father is going to let junior help out at the office, the split school year no longer serves any purpose. Free day care or not, at least the kid is in a familiar, constructive, supervised environment, rather than at home alone playing "Postal Worker" or "Steely Eyed Baby Killer" or whatever on Nintendo, watching gangster rappers kill cops, do drugs, and disrespect women on MTV while mom and dad work 12 hours a day so they can keep up with the Jones'es. Good parenting starts LONG before school ever comes into the picture. I think full year schools is a great idea that's long overdue. And trust me, it takes alot for me to say that, 'cause if you think I LIKE following a school bus that stops every 10 feet to pick up a kid at his front door, think again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/mad.gif"> I HATE that. But I think it's worth the inconvenience.<BR>And just so you know, NO, I have no children, however, I WAS a child once, and I know what I did for three months while my parents worked during the day. I would have been ALOT better off being in school. I wouldn't have liked it, but then, life ain't all about making you happy. Sometimes, we have to make choices that're going to piss off our kids, and inconvenience us. Welcome to the real world.<P>-Taiji||||reg||4
Z||000005||fiero56||08-14-2000||03:02 PM||||I'm in college now, but never got the year round school year. When in high-school my freshman year, I decided to take summer school(well, parents didn't like the D+ in my algebra 2 class, so I got to do it again over the summer). After the first bit of griping, and anger left, I really loved the summer school bit, and voluntarily took it the next two years. It was because I spent 5 days a week in the same class for 4 hours straight. I couldn't stand doing the 50 minute class session, it was so split up. Tests were weeks apart, chapters so spread out. In summer school, those 4 hours straight were awsome. We did 2-3 chapters a day, took a test for each chapter covered everyday, did 2-3 chapters worth of homework each day. I learned more in those summer school classes than I ever learned in regular school. I was always straight A+ in summer school, over the regular C+-B- in regular school. <BR>In college, I try to get the classes that meet the fewest times a week, since they are always the longest, 2-3 hours. I do better in those classes, than the ones that meet everyday, but are only 60 minutes long. <BR>Thats my viewpoint. Perfect world for me would be school 8 hours a day, 2 classes a day, each 4 hours long with the same amount of work being done each class as would normally be done.||||reg||1
Z||000006||frontal lobe||08-14-2000||03:32 PM||||I have 3 daughters in school now:  9, 7, and 5.  I am sure originally the idea of summer vacation was that the children had to work on the farm in the summer.  However, I still like my children having off in the summer, even though I don't having them working in the fields.  I want to have as much influence on them as possible.  My wife stays home with them, and teaches them all kinds of different things during the summer.  It's a great time for them.  Plus, they don't have to go to bed as early so I see them for a longer time at night.<P>For those with 2 working parents, the solution is simple:  quit spending so much money and have the mother stay home and invest in her children instead.  Quit looking to society, schools, government to raise your children.  I don't try to tax others to baby-sit my kids.  Shame on those of you that do that--tax me to pay for your child care.  You had them, you raise them.  (I understand there are occasional extenuating circumstances where both parents HAVE to work, but usually it's just that they both want to for the money.  Let the flames begin.)<P>One more thought.  I was able to get summer jobs my last 3 years in high school that provided a substantial head start for funds for college.  I would guess it would be easier for some teachers to take summer jobs if they had a block of time, too.<P>So my vote is to keep the summer break.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cooter||08-14-2000||03:45 PM||||Very good points everybody- keep them coming!<P>Thank you,<BR>Greg||||reg||1
Z||000008||fropuff||08-14-2000||05:51 PM||||Being in 10th grade in a school without a year-long schedule, I don't think I'd like it at all.  But, I haven't experienced it either.  I think the regular schedule is just fine the way it is.  We go about 6 1/2 hours a day, 5 days a week; no more.  The only thing that I would maybe like better is something called "block scheduling."  It is where you go for 7 hours a day.  You go to three classes for 2 hours, and get 20 minutes between.  That would be 7 hours a day, 4 days a week.  That's right, 4 days a week.  However, school lasts 10 months, not 9 like ours.  ?????????   ||||reg||10
Z||000009||blayde8||08-14-2000||08:43 PM||||I'm going to be a soph. in high-school to and I hate the idea of year-round school.  Then you have really nothing to look forward to and it will get really boring if you have school all year.  I am happy with the current school year, but they could chop off a few more days to make it a little shorter.||||reg||13
Z||000010||2birds||08-14-2000||11:37 PM||||Tell it like you feel it, frontal lobe.  That's what it's for.<BR>I certainly understand the concept of summer work time for the kids.  Remember detasseling, anyone?  That and many others haven't gone away yet.  Employers are fighting over teenagers these days, and not just for minimum wage. <BR>My point is similar to frontal's in that the parents should be teaching their children during the off time from school, and no watching Rikki, Sally Jesse, and Montel on the idiot's lantern all day. Whether it's History and Geography on vacation, or doing projects at home or learning how to swim, the things parents teach their kids are just as important to them as the school teacher's work.  <BR>I'm thankful that my job in the Navy allows my wife the oportunity to raise our sons.  I hear it all the time; "Gotta get the wife back to work so we can have more money."  For what, a new Expedition every two years, or a new computer every time Intel sneezes, or to buy Junior and Juniette a bunch of gotta-haves" because they don't want the hassle of a summer job? I don't want my kids raised by a "provider", who only cares about the extra income her family is getting while she sits and watches the highly educational programming I mentioned earlier.<BR>If I couldn't afford to live on one salary, I wouldn't have had kids, so I can't claim extenuating circumstances.  At least try to live within your means, instead of missing out on your children.  I grew up poor for money, but never had a lack of family.  I like the extra hour or two to spend with them before bedtime in the summer (That's why I'm usually on the board so late at night).||||reg||1
Z||000011||fierospeeder||08-14-2000||11:56 PM||||Japan is already in the lead of technology.<BR>wait nevermind.<P>Im in college right now, either the 5 or 6 year plan.  My gpa dropped because of being burned out from doing classes that were required by my university.  humanities, social science etc... while the interest of computer science was what i want to study.  I have to take community college for a year, because my gpa dropped below a C.  The comm, college im going to, is suppose to be the largest in the state.  The tuition is 30 dollars per class hour, a lot cheaper then a university.  Picking classes are a lot easier and everything seems so much better then my university.  It runs on quarters instead of semesters.  I know this school is something i look foward too because im just taking classes I WANT.  Also i can take some auto mechanics classes, because its in the curriculum, and some welding woohoo.<P>As a kid, I always hated school, except for gym, music, and art. Unless you had a hot teacher then i looked foward to class.  Extending school year round is a bad idea for kids.  You will be burning them out and taking their childhood away, unless they have bad parents and they rather be in school.   <P>In most highschools, summer school is optional, if you want to get ahead or to make up work.  Junior and grade schools also do have summer class but are only held for 2 hours to help students out that are having trouble.  Students do bad in school, and it varies from bad teachers to lack of interest.  Forcing them to stay longer isn't really an answer.  <P>I stayed home by myself at the age of 7.  Not all kids are that responsible to do that.  You can try changing shifts to stay home with your child, unless have a parent or let them stay at their friends house.  That is usually the best thing, and what most kids do when school is out.  To hand with friends.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||JohnsFiero||08-15-2000||01:09 AM||||After lurking around the forum for the past few weeks (great place), I felt I needed to add my two cents.  <P>I have one daughter (12) with whom I have noticed the following pattern during school -she goes to school for 9 months, has 3 months off for summer, goes back to school, spends the next 3 months reviewing what she forgot over summer vacation, and subsequently only gets 6 months of new material.  Not to mention all the teacher in-service days, national holidays (since when did Arbor Day become a day off from school), etc., I think year-round school is a great idea.  Several of the school districts in this area have already moved to such a schedule with the biggest complaint from parents being less family vacation time during the summer (since when did families take 12 weeks for vacation during the summer?!)  <P>The lack of year-round school in our district played a large role in our decision to homeschool our daughter.  We have a year-round schedule with the same amount of vacation time, but it is divided throughout the year - two weeks for spring break, etc.  Fortunately, my wife and I have the luxury of both working at home so this is easily accomplished.  <P>I think the issue cuts much deeper than if the schools are being used as babysitters; the question is are the academic goals that we achieved during school still being accomplished with the status quo (having gone to the same school as my daughter before we took her out 15 years earlier, I can attetst that they are not).  <P>Obviously there is no right or wrong answer, but with the way things are now, there is a definite need for improvement and year-round school may be the beginning.  Just my $0.02.  <P>John    ||||reg||1
Z||000013||2birds||08-15-2000||02:08 AM||||Good points, IMHO, and welcome to the Forum.<BR>While I don't believe homeschooling is the perfect solution (at least not for my kids yet), I do agree there should be a standard of achievement, and paths to improvement.  <BR>We took basic skills tests (Iowa Tests of Basic Skills, Iowa Tests of Educational Development, Armed Services Vocational Aptitude Battery, etc).  While a lot of people don't like them for various reasons, at least they are a tool to be used and interpreted.  The ASVAB is even free.  I took pride in my scores, and in improving them each year.  The big thing I see wrong with them is creating an environment where the tests will be valid (no distractions, pumping the kids up so they want to do well, and no coaching from people who have a stake in the results).<BR>Give the educators some credit, too.  When I was a Navy recruiter, I saw some of what they deal with.  Large student to teacher ratio's (not every kid learns the same way or speed), parental and student indifference, belief that education is a right and not a privilege, lack of supplies, and in some cases, significantly lower pay than other college grads.  Good support for home schooling, if the parents have the resources. <BR>As far as burnout goes, that and financial reasons sent my brother and I into the Navy.  Not everyone is ready for college, for whatever reason, but I made it through the Navy's Nuclear Power School with the motivators of not failing and it was MY JOB.  Toughest thing I ever did.<BR>Obviously, there is no perfect solution for every kid, family, and school system, but a little innovation is usually a good thing.  My parents chose small town Iowa for educational and environmental reasons, instead of economic ones, and gritted it out until my brother and I finished high school.  Over the long haul, we've done fine.  My (limited) experience so far with small town Georgia schools has been good, although I wish the high schools were a bit smaller.  <BR>And a four legged canine isn't spelled or pronounced DAWG anywhere else but here... ||||reg||1
Z||000014||DJRice||08-15-2000||09:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, lucidatypewriter">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 2birds:<BR><B>Pay the teachers what they are worth, considering the results we are expecting, and don't rely on them to replace us in our parental roles...  </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Im not exactly sure what to make of this particular part of your statement.  Certainly you are not saying that teachers are <B><I>overcompensated</B></I>??  I am the child of two educators.  My Dad has 30 years in education, 22 of which were spent as a Junior High/Middle School Principal.  My mother is a retired Middle School Math/Algebra teacher.  I make as much money with my entry level engineering job as my mother did after 30 years in education.  Teachers are expected to take their work home with them, they certainly are not compensated enough.  More and more new teachers (at least in Florida) are leaving the profession after 1 year or less because they simply cant pay the bills.  <P>Bottom line: You get what you pay for, regardless of how you configure the school year.  Until this country quits paying its pubilc school educators like they are nothing but mass day care providers, I dont think the educational system is going to improve.<P>------------------<BR>Dillon - Titusville, FL<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000015||frontal lobe||08-15-2000||01:06 PM||||I'll try to not get Cooter's thread too far off his question.  Regarding the review time after the end of the summer, it is true that it is an issue.  But again, the parents can minimize that.  For example, my girls are going into 4th and 3rd grade.  They have been reading throughout the summer so no problem there, but if they had not been, we would have started them reading again about now.  Last week, my girls had to start doing math flash cards again every morning.  They HATE doing them (which I can't understand, because on the box it says a FUN way to learn math facts--I remind my girls that it says it, too, but they aren't buying it.  Hehe.) but too bad.  Time to get ready for school again.  So there is a way around the review problem, but poor teachers wouldn't DARE solve it by sending out review sheets and suggestions for parents to start with the kids a couple of weeks before school started.  Cooter, could you IMAGINE the reaction from parents that would get!!!  That parents would actually have to DO some work with their own kids.  Why!!!!  That's what we pay those teachers ALL THAT MONEY for.<P>Oh oh, I'm starting to rant again.  <P>Final word.  I can tell 2birds doesn't think teachers are overpaid.  Neither do I.  I'm FOR teachers.  My wife goes in 1 day a week to the school and helps out a teacher.  I just wish teachers would be allowed to teach and not try to force them to be parents or social workers.||||reg||1
Z||000016||rickb||08-16-2000||09:25 PM||||Cooter, I teach at a college and we do not have any time off.  I belive the biggest advantage is the students don't forget things from one class to another.  The bad thing on my part is we don't even have 2 weeks off here, 1 week off there, It's 4 days a week 52 weeks a year (well we do get a couple extra days off on Christmas and New Year)||||reg||1
Z||000017||2birds||08-17-2000||02:28 AM||||Sorry it took me so long to get back to this (Clutch fun and games).  <BR>Frontal is right, I don't think teachers are overpaid at all.  Some, but certainly not all.  Considering what we are asking teachers to do for 13-14 years of our kids formative years, we need to keep the best we can.  Sometimes enjoying your job just isn't enough.  The district I lived in when I recruited in Minnesota started teachers at around 21k a year (1993), according to their newsletter.  I made that with under two years in the Navy (1987), and no degree, and that was without my bonuses and Sea, Sub, and Nuke Pays included.<P>Anybody know much about block scheduling?  From my limited understanding of it, it sounds like you spend more time on a subject each day, and finish it sooner, instead of dragging it through the whole year.  ||||reg||1
Z||000018||DJRice||08-17-2000||09:02 AM||||Block Scheduling is set up so you spend about 2 hours in each class, but only go to half as many classes each day.  You alternate classes each day as well.  You end up with the same amount of hours per week in each class, but you get then 2 hours at a time instead of 1 hour at a time.<P>------------------<BR>Dillon - Titusville, FL<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"You mean like the back seat of a Volkswagen?"||||reg||1
Z||000019||2birds||08-17-2000||10:56 PM||||Thanks, Dillon.<BR>Today, on NPR's Talk of the Nation, one of the topics was homework, whether or not kids have too much homework, how much parents should be involved in helping, etc.  My 6 year old had homework starting in Kindergarten, took a half-hour max each day.  Most of it was just practicing what he did in school during the day.  I think it was mostly used to guage how much attention the parents paid to what their kids were doing.  Seemed to me that repetition was how I learned in math and science, while writing papers and reading helped bring it all together.  Most college courses I have taken required decent writing skills, at the least.  Repetition again.  So there's an argument for at least keeping something going during the time off between grades.  You could do it as homework, but if you blow off homework during the school year (and get away with it), why would you be expected to do it during the summer? ||||reg||1
